  "challenges": [
    {
      "publicId": "chal_quiz_sdm_231",
      "name": "progress-aggregates-quiz",
      "type": "quiz",
      "title": "Read the progress numbers the way SQLite computes them",
      "description": "Two results from the CodeDev progress incident: what COUNT counts after a LEFT JOIN, and what integer division does to a percentage. Decide whether each statement is true or false.",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "outcomes": ["queries"],
      "points": 10,
      "questions": [
        {
          "type": "single_choice",
          "points": 5,
          "question": "courses has two rows: (10, 'SQL Basics') and (40, 'Kubernetes Basics'). lessons has three rows, all with course_id = 10. Course 40 has no lessons yet.\n\nSELECT c.course_id, COUNT(*) AS total_lessons\nFROM courses AS c\nLEFT JOIN lessons AS l ON l.course_id = c.course_id\nGROUP BY c.course_id\nORDER BY c.course_id;\n\nStatement: \"The row for course 40 shows total_lessons = 0, because course 40 has no lessons.\"",
          "explanation": "False. The LEFT JOIN keeps course 40 as one row whose lessons columns are all NULL. COUNT(*) counts rows, not lessons, so course 40 gets total_lessons = 1 (course 10 gets 3). COUNT(l.lesson_id) skips NULL values and returns 0 for course 40, which is the number you wanted. The common misconception is that COUNT(*) counts matches: it counts every row in the group, including the all-NULL row a LEFT JOIN adds for a parent without children.",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "True",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "False",
              "correct": true
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "single_choice",
          "points": 5,
          "question": "Mai has finished 2 of the 3 lessons of SQL Basics. The progress query computes the percentage on SQLite, where both counts are integers:\n\nSELECT 2 / 3 * 100 AS a,\n       2 * 100 / 3 AS b;\n\nStatement: \"a is 0 and b is 66.\"",
          "explanation": "True. When both operands are integers, SQLite's / is integer division and drops the fraction. In a, 2 / 3 is 0 and 0 * 100 is 0, so every learner who has not finished the whole course sees 0%. In b, 2 * 100 is 200 and 200 / 3 is 66, the percentage rounded down. PostgreSQL behaves the same way for integer operands. Writing 100.0 instead of 100 switches to real division (66.66...), and ROUND would then give 67, not 66. A common misconception is that SQL division always returns a fraction, as it does in JavaScript.",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "True",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "False",
              "correct": false
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
